BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:youth-music-trailblazer-fund-round-13-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260828T132446Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260828
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260829
SUMMARY:Deadline: Youth Music: Trailblazer Fund (Round 13)
DESCRIPTION:Youth Music's Trailblazer Fund gives GBP 2\,000 to GBP 30\,000 
 to organisations in England running projects that let children and young p
 eople aged 25 or under make\, learn and earn in music. A project should do
  at least one of three things: trial work or test a new way of working\, s
 ustain a grassroots programme\, or disrupt the status quo. Doing all three
  is welcome. THEMES: your work must meet one of these: early years\; Disab
 led\, d/Deaf and neurodivergent young people\; the youth justice system\; 
 young people facing barriers\; young adults\; or organisations and the wor
 kforce. WHAT IT LOOKS LIKE IN PRACTICE\, using Youth Music's own examples:
  a new CIC testing ways to help nurseries embed music-making into the day\
 ; a small grassroots music organisation sustaining a programme that alread
 y works\; a Music Education Hub setting up a Youth Board with real budget 
 and decision-making power over how the hub is run\; or an established yout
 h centre trying a music programme for the first time. ROUND 13: opened 31 
 July 2026\, deadline 28 August 2026 at 5pm\, notification 11 December 2026
 \, for projects starting between January and March 2027. ROUND 14: opens 2
 3 October 2026\, deadline 20 November 2026 at 5pm\, notification 19 March 
 2027\, for projects starting between April and June 2027. All deadlines ar
 e 5pm. NOTE: this is a grant to ORGANISATIONS\, not to individual musician
 s or composers.\n\nOrganisation: Youth Music\nLocation: England only. Proj
 ects for children and young people aged 25 or under.\nAward: GBP 2\,000 to
  GBP 30\,000 for organisations in England. No application fee.\nDuration: 
 Round 13 projects start between January and March 2027. Round opened 31 Ju
 ly 2026\; deadline 28 August 2026 at 5pm\; notification 11 December 2026.\
 n\nMore: https://www.youthmusic.org.uk/funding/i-need-funding/trailblazer-
 fund?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.youthmusic.org.uk/funding/i-need-funding/trailblazer-fund?u
 tm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:England only. Projects for children and young people aged 25 or un
 der.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:abbey-foundation-day-use-studio-grant-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260828T132446Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261201
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261202
SUMMARY:Deadline: Abbey Foundation for the Arts: Day Use Studio Grant (Jan 
 to Jun 2027)
DESCRIPTION:The Abbey Foundation for the Arts in Boonville\, California awa
 rds six-month day-use studio residencies paired with a materials stipend o
 f up to USD 5\,000 for equipment and supplies needed to realise the propos
 ed project. The studio is 850 square feet indoors with an additional 850 s
 quare foot screened outdoor area for large-scale work\; note that it may b
 e shared with one other artist during some periods. ELIGIBILITY: open to a
 rtists across all mediums. IMPORTANT: this is a day-use studio grant\, not
  a live-in residency\, and the foundation provides no support for housing\
 , transportation\, meals or travel\, so it states the grant is most approp
 riate for artists already living in the local counties. CONDITIONS: at the
  end of the residency the artist is expected to contribute one of the piec
 es made while in residence to the foundation\, and the programme may come 
 to require grant recipients to take part in community workshops as it deve
 lops. DEADLINES: 1 December 2026 for the 1 January to 30 June 2027 residen
 cy\; the foundation runs the call twice a year\, with a 1 June deadline fo
 r the following July to December period. No application fee.\n\nOrganisati
 on: Abbey Foundation for the Arts\nLocation: Boonville\, California\, Unit
 ed States (Anderson Valley\, Mendocino County). Day use of the studio only
 : there is NO support for housing\, transportation\, meals or travel\, so 
 the foundation says it suits artists already living in the surrounding cou
 nties.\nAward: Stipend of up to USD 5\,000 for the purchase of materials a
 nd additional equipment needed to complete the proposed project\, plus six
  months of day use of an 850 square foot studio and a further 850 square f
 oot screened outdoor area for large-scale work. No housing\, travel\, meal
 s or transport support. No application fee.\nDuration: Six-month residency
 \, 1 January to 30 June 2027\n\nMore: https://www.abbeyfoundationfortheart
 s.org/grants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.abbeyfoundationforthearts.org/grants?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Boonville\, California\, United States (Anderson Valley\, Mendocin
 o County). Day use of the studio only: there is NO support for housing\, t
 ransportation\, meals or travel\, so the foundation says it suits artists 
 already living in the surrounding counties.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:art-worker-artist-grant-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260828T132446Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260914
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260915
SUMMARY:Deadline: Art Worker Artist Grant 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Art Worker Artist Grant is a biennial award for artists who
 se livelihoods are connected to the production\, handling\, exhibition\, c
 are\, movement or stewardship of art. Its premise is that a great many art
 ists fund their practice through labour inside the art industry itself\, a
 nd that this work rarely comes with support for their own artistic develop
 ment. WHO IS ELIGIBLE: artists who support their practice by providing any
  form of labour in any area of the art industry\, including but not limite
 d to museums\, galleries\, artist studios\, collections\, auction houses\,
  art fairs\, fabrication\, framing and mountmaking workshops\, conservatio
 n labs\, art logistics and crating companies\, and storage facilities. WHA
 T IT FUNDS: the development of artistic work in whatever form that takes\,
  with the money explicitly unrestricted\; the call lists studio time\, tra
 vel\, healthcare\, research\, equipment\, rest\, materials\, living expens
 es and childcare as legitimate uses. There are no required outcomes and no
  required acquittal\, so no reporting obligation. APPLICATION: intentional
 ly minimal\, consisting of a brief statement describing how the funds coul
 d support artistic development or production\, and a link to a website\, p
 ortfolio\, social media or other documentation of the work. SELECTION: by 
 an independent jury of Andria Hickey (curator and museum leader\, curatori
 al advisor for the Barclays Center art programme in Brooklyn\, previously 
 at The Shed\, Pace Gallery and MOCA Cleveland)\, Clynton Lowry (artist and
  publisher) and Paul Graham (photographer). IMPORTANT: applications for th
 e 2026 edition are capped at the first 500 submissions\, so the call may c
 lose before the stated deadline. Key dates in AEST: entries open 24 August
  2026\, entries close 14 September 2026\, recipient announced 2 November 2
 026. Overseen and funded by ARTIST ENGINEERING.\n\nOrganisation: ARTIST EN
 GINEERING\nLocation: Remote. No geographic restriction is stated in the ca
 ll\; key dates are given in AEST.\nAward: AUD 5\,000 to one recipient for 
 the 2026 edition. Funds are unrestricted: they may be used for studio time
 \, travel\, healthcare\, research\, equipment\, rest\, materials\, living 
 expenses\, childcare\, or something else entirely. There are no required o
 utcomes and no required grant acquittal. No application fee is mentioned.\
 nDuration: One-off unrestricted grant\, awarded biennially\n\nMore: https:
 //artistengineering.co/pages/awa?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm
 _medium=referral
URL:https://artistengineering.co/pages/awa?utm_source=artificialnouveaugran
 tdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote. No geographic restriction is stated in the call\; key date
 s are given in AEST.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:toronto-arts-council-artists-in-the-library-2027@artificialnouveau.gith
 ub.io
DTSTAMP:20260828T132446Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261006
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261007
SUMMARY:Deadline: Artists in the Library 2027 (Toronto Arts Council)
DESCRIPTION:Created in 2014 as a partnership between Toronto Arts Council a
 nd Toronto Public Library\, Artists in the Library gives Toronto artists p
 aid opportunities and space while widening access to the arts in Etobicoke
 \, York\, North York\, East York and Scarborough. It funds professional ar
 tists\, arts organisations and collectives in any artistic discipline to c
 reate and deliver free community-engaged arts programming at a Toronto Pub
 lic Library branch. TAC encourages applications from its equity priority g
 roups and from artists working in their local communities. WHAT RECIPIENTS
  GET OUT OF IT: a chance to explore their practice in a community setting\
 , access to branch space and facilities\, meaningful engagement with the b
 ranch and its community\, close support from the branch team\, experience 
 navigating a multi-stakeholder environment\, and the delivery of a communi
 ty-engaged arts project from start to finish. WHAT COUNTS: community engag
 ement is required but its format can vary\, covering exhibitions\, install
 ations\, performances\, concerts\, screenings\, readings\, filmmaking\, st
 orytelling\, workshops\, artist talks\, participatory programmes\, arts ed
 ucation and mentorship. Permanent exhibitions and installations are not el
 igible. Projects are produced by the recipient\, hosted and supported by t
 he assigned branch\, and must be delivered on site within branch operating
  hours. SCALE: because host slots at TPL branches are limited\, this is no
 t for one-off workshops or small-scale events\; the offering must be subst
 antial with a budget to match. LOCATION: there is no list of eligible bran
 ches for 2026. Applicants indicate a preferred area or neighbourhood outsi
 de the downtown core and TPL staff assign the branch based on suitability 
 and availability\, so a different branch than the one requested may be ass
 igned. For Toronto and East York\, downtown is excluded\, defined as south
  of St Clair\, east of Jane and west of the Don Valley. Only one branch ma
 y be proposed. ELIGIBILITY: individuals must be Canadian citizens\, perman
 ent residents\, have a pending PR application\, or be a protected person\,
  must hold a Social Insurance Number\, and must have lived in the City of 
 Toronto for at least one year before the deadline and live and work there 
 at least eight months a year (no PO boxes). Artists in graduate study may 
 not apply for work related to their programme without a letter from the pr
 ogramme director. Organisations must be incorporated non-profit arts organ
 isations\, and collectives must operate on a non-profit basis\, be located
  in Toronto\, and have both members (for a pair) or a majority of members 
 resident in Toronto\; successful collectives must supply bank account deta
 ils in the collective's name. IMPORTANT: proposals must align with TPL's p
 rogramming policy\, and proposals that TPL staff judge unsuitable for a br
 anch or impossible in its context will not be funded\, so read the 'Workin
 g With Toronto Public Library' section of the guidelines. First-time appli
 cants are encouraged to consult the programme manager and to attend the Zo
 om office hours on eligibility and project fit (17 September) and on budge
 ts (29 September). Contact: Lena Golubtsova\, lena@torontoartscouncil.org.
  Deadline 6 October 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Toronto Arts Council\, in partn
 ership with Toronto Public Library\nLocation: Toronto\, Canada. Programmin
 g takes place at a Toronto Public Library branch outside the downtown core
 \, in Etobicoke York\, North York\, Toronto and East York\, or Scarborough
 . Applicants must be City of Toronto residents.\nAward: Maximum grant of C
 AD 20\,000\, which may cover up to 100 percent of project costs. Payment o
 f professional artist fees is a requirement of the programme. Recipients a
 lso get access to space and facilities at the assigned library branch for 
 arts programming and presentation\, and support from the branch team. Fund
 ed by the City of Toronto. No application fee is mentioned.\nDuration: Pro
 gramming must take place between September and December 2027\n\nMore: http
 s://torontoartscouncil.org/grants/artists-in-the-library/?utm_source=artif
 icialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://torontoartscouncil.org/grants/artists-in-the-library/?utm_sourc
 e=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Toronto\, Canada. Programming takes place at a Toronto Public Libr
 ary branch outside the downtown core\, in Etobicoke York\, North York\, To
 ronto and East York\, or Scarborough. Applicants must be City of Toronto r
 esidents.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:laselle-travel-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260828T132446Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Dorothy Antoinette LaSelle Travel Fellowship 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Dorothy Antoinette LaSelle Foundation established this fell
 owship in recognition of the impact travel can have on an artist's career.
  Two awards of USD 5\,000 go to artists working and residing in Texas\, en
 abling each recipient to complete a research trip that furthers their arti
 stic practice. The fellowship echoes its namesake's own history: Dorothy A
 ntoinette 'Toni' LaSelle\, a pioneer of Modernism in Texas\, travelled to 
 Europe in 1927-28 after completing her MA at the University of Chicago\, e
 xploring the art of England\, Italy and France on a trip made possible by 
 a study fund awarded for work in the First Student Art Exhibition at the R
 enaissance Society in 1926. JUROR: this year's guest juror is Lana Meador\
 , Associate Curator of Modern and Contemporary Art at the San Antonio Muse
 um of Art\, who joined SAMA's curatorial department in 2015 and has organi
 sed more than a dozen exhibitions there\, including co-curating Texas Wome
 n: A New History of Abstract Art (2020) and the ongoing Gateway series of 
 commissioned site-specific murals. She has juried artist awards for the Ci
 ty of San Antonio's Department of Arts and Culture\, the International Col
 or Awards\, Texas Sculpture Group and the Utah Artist Fellowship. ELIGIBIL
 ITY: must currently reside in Texas and have lived there for the past year
 \; must be a practising professional with at least three solo or group exh
 ibitions in the past three years (BFA and juried exhibitions count)\; and 
 must currently hold a degree in the arts. APPLICATION MATERIALS: image thu
 mbnails of a maximum of four works with title\, date\, medium and dimensio
 ns (JPEG or JPG only\, at least 1\,200 pixels on the longest side and unde
 r 5MB each)\; an artist CV\; a reference contact list with two names\, con
 tact details and relationship to the artist\; a project proposal of 2\,000
  characters or less covering why the fellowship matters to your practice\,
  your goals for the travel\, and when the proposed travel would take place
 \; and a copy of a valid Texas driver's licence or other proof of one year
  of Texas residency. All information and communications are in English\, w
 ith translation assistance available on request. Applications opened 3 Aug
 ust 2026\; awardees are notified 2 November 2026. Questions: info@lasellef
 oundation.org. Deadline 31 August 2026 at 11:59pm CDT.\n\nOrganisation: Do
 rothy Antoinette LaSelle Foundation\nLocation: Texas\, USA. Applicants mus
 t currently reside in Texas and have lived there for the past year. The fu
 nded research trip itself may be anywhere.\nAward: Two awards of USD 5\,00
 0 each\, distributed in the fall of 2026\, to fund a research trip that fu
 rthers the artist's practice. No application fee.\nDuration: Travel must b
 e completed between 1 December 2026 and 31 December 2027\n\nMore: https://
 www.lasellefoundation.org/travel-fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.lasellefoundation.org/travel-fellowship?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Texas\, USA. Applicants must currently reside in Texas and have li
 ved there for the past year. The funded research trip itself may be anywhe
 re.
END:VEVENT
